I think that it really depends on what one's goal's are.  There really
is (I hope) more to music than just selling records.  While Madonna
might somehow become a footnote in music history, there are lots
of other people who sold records, made money . . . and are now derided.
(Really, would you really want to listen to an Andy Gibb record? or
the Bee Gees?  Or Micheal Jackson in a few years?)  

>> 
>> To my mind, selling sex is no different than selling any other commodity
>> you may have.
>> 
>> "Faking a relationship" is the one thing Max mentions that I also find
>> wrong, because it implies failure to fulfill a contract.  If both parties
>> recognize and agree on the finite and voluntary nature of the exchange --
>> that the sex-seller can stop paying sex when s/he no longer needs what the
>> sex-buyer is providing in return -- I don't see a problem with "playing
>> house" either.
>>                                         dV/dt

I think that there are two points here that are becoming muddled.
One is "social reliablility"; the other is "honor."   Contracts can
be recognized for either or both reasons...   With "reliability,"
one honors a contract for the former reason, it is because there is
an external threat of retribution.  However, if this was removed,
the contract would not be valid simply because noone could really enforce
it.  "Honor" implies more of a _covenential_, rather than _contractual_
, form of relationship.    I think that is is important to recognize
that sometimes agreements and beliefs really are "til death do us part";
would one really want a spouse operating under a "til I find a higher bidder"
principle?

dV/dt confuses these by assuming that a person in a relationship
is under a covenant to reveal his or her true intentions, be they
contractual or covenant.   A person operating only under "social reliability"
, however, would not have to agree internally to _any_ agreement (ie,
they could "fake it") so long as they only break their social 
contracts when the other party is unable to muster retribution of 
some kind.

I would suggest that it is psycholigically easier for most people 
to live without contractual agreements, than it would be to live
without those based upon covenants.

             John


